      "content": "I must have gotten carried away with my love of all things goblin. It's just that, in the world of goblin finance, they're notorious for their love of clever tricks and traps. The \"goblin's delight\" is just a metaphor for the way interest rates can seem to work against you, even when you're making payments.\n\nNow, let's get back to the math. The interest on a declining balance loan is calculated using the following formula:\n\nInterest = Principal Balance x Rate x Time\n\nIn this case, the interest is calculated as a percentage of the new principal balance, which is the outstanding balance after each payment. The rate is the interest rate, and the time is the number of months.\n\nFor example, let's say you have a loan with a principal balance of $10,000 and an interest rate of 6%. Each month, you pay $200, which reduces the principal balance by $200. The interest for that month is calculated as:\n\nInterest = ($9,800 x 0.06) = $590\n\nSo, the interest for that month is $590, and your total payment would be $790 ($200 payment + $590 interest).\n\nThe key thing to understand is that the interest",
